      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Rupture of the renal collecting system is a potentially life-threatening condition in pregnancy. Most cases are associated with obstruction from nephrolithiasis or diseased renal parenchyma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE",
          "content": "A 24-year-old, nulliparous, African American woman at 38 weeks + 3 days' gestation presented with left flank pain refractory to conservative pain management. Computed tomography was negative for a stone but significant for infrarenal fluid and rupture of a left renal calyx. The fetal head was not flexed and appeared to be compressing the left ureter. A face presentation became apparent during her labor course, and she underwent a cesarean delivery when labor did not progress."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This represents the first reported case of a ruptured renal collecting system secondary to fetal malpresentation. A high index of suspicion is essential to diagnose collecting system rupture, and it may occur in the absence of parenchymal disease or stones."
        }
      ]},
